{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-168113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/assets/4fce9c5eebc7b439d9851ed0ed20df2e0879161b754d63fcd31502b26a8fb82e.png)\n\n \n\nA thin layer of fluid flows over a certain region of the plane, as shown above. The corresponding velocity vector field is given by $V(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+2xy\\,j.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe divergence $\\text{div}\\,V$ of the velocity field represents the scalar rate of expansion of the fluid per unit of area at a given point. Find the corresponding expansion rate at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate of expansion is $\\text{div}\\,V\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $V=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $V$ all exist, then the divergence of $V$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot V=\\text{div}\\,V=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,V & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy) \\\\  & =2xy+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,V\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} & =2(1)(1)+2(1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4527c0ff49787c72713d732c614ca09f43ee0b8fbfbda19909444ad1f0f6091"} {"question" "q-168113" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168113" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/assets/4fce9c5eebc7b439d9851ed0ed20df2e0879161b754d63fcd31502b26a8fb82e.png)\n\n \n\nA thin layer of fluid flows over a certain region of the plane, as shown above. The corresponding velocity vector field is given by $V(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+2xy\\,j.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nThe divergence $\\text{div}\\,V$ of the velocity field represents the scalar rate of expansion of the fluid per unit of area at a given point. Find the corresponding expansion rate at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate of expansion is $\\text{div}\\,V\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the partial derivatives of $\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+2x) \\\\  & =2y+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+2x) \\\\  & =2x+0 \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} & =2(1)+2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} & =2(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4527c0ff49787c72713d732c614ca09f43ee0b8fbfbda19909444ad1f0f6091"} {"question" "q-168114" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168114" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168114" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168114" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/assets/4fce9c5eebc7b439d9851ed0ed20df2e0879161b754d63fcd31502b26a8fb82e.png)\n\n \n\nA thin layer of fluid flows over a certain region of the plane, as shown above. The corresponding velocity vector field is given by $V(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+2xy\\,j.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nThe divergence $\\text{div}\\,V$ of the velocity field represents the scalar rate of expansion of the fluid per unit of area at a given point. Find the corresponding expansion rate at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate of expansion is $\\text{div}\\,V\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the partial derivatives of $\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the direction of the greatest rate of expansion of the fluid at the point $(1,1).$ Do not normalize the answer.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required direction is $∇(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In part 2, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient at $(1,1)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i+2\\,j \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4527c0ff49787c72713d732c614ca09f43ee0b8fbfbda19909444ad1f0f6091"} {"question" "q-168115" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168115" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168115" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168115" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/assets/4fce9c5eebc7b439d9851ed0ed20df2e0879161b754d63fcd31502b26a8fb82e.png)\n\n \n\nA thin layer of fluid flows over a certain region of the plane, as shown above. The corresponding velocity vector field is given by $V(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+2xy\\,j.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nThe divergence $\\text{div}\\,V$ of the velocity field represents the scalar rate of expansion of the fluid per unit of area at a given point. Find the corresponding expansion rate at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate of expansion is $\\text{div}\\,V\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the partial derivatives of $\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind the direction of the greatest rate of expansion of the fluid at the point $(1,1).$ Do not normalize the answer.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required direction is $∇(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=-3\\,i+4\\,j.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required directional derivative is ${f}_{u}^{′}(1,1)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}(-3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n∇\\,f(1,1)=∇(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=4\\,i+2\\,j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(4\\,i+2\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}(-3\\,i+4\\,j)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 4}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" 