 {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168117" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 1, we found that \n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\text{div}\\,V\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=4.\n$$\n\nAnd from part 3, we know that the gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇f(1,1)=∇(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=⟨4,\\,2⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =4+4(x-1)+2(y-1) \\\\  & =4x+2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168117" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4527c0ff49787c72713d732c614ca09f43ee0b8fbfbda19909444ad1f0f6091"} {"question" "q-168117" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168117" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x+2y-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/assets/4fce9c5eebc7b439d9851ed0ed20df2e0879161b754d63fcd31502b26a8fb82e.png)\n\n \n\nA thin layer of fluid flows over a certain region of the plane, as shown above. The corresponding velocity vector field is given by $V(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+2xy\\,j.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nThe divergence $\\text{div}\\,V$ of the velocity field represents the scalar rate of expansion of the fluid per unit of area at a given point. Find the corresponding expansion rate at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate of expansion is $\\text{div}\\,V\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the partial derivatives of $\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind the direction of the greatest rate of expansion of the fluid at the point $(1,1).$ Do not normalize the answer.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required direction is $∇(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFind the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=-3\\,i+4\\,j.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required directional derivative is ${f}_{u}^{′}(1,1)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{4}{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the best linear approximation of the function $f(x,y)=\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required linear approximation is $f(x,y)≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4x+2y-2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe scalar curl of a plane velocity field is called the **vorticity,** denoted $\\omega .$ The vorticity describes a measure of the local rotation of the field at a given point. Find the vorticity of $V$ at $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\omega (1,1)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"question" "q-168118" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $V=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $V$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $V$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $P={x}^{2}y$ and $Q=2xy.$ Therefore, the scalar curl (i.e., the vorticity) of $V$ at $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega  & =\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2y-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega (1,1) & =(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =2(1)-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/state.json" "file_sha256" "4923cb5b1a93f927c9eb544c2a41064c9f708f522dcca4bacd8096bcf43a16bb"} {"